  "Number": 17,
  "Question": "The number of distinct points common to the curves \\(x^2+4y^2=1\\) and \\(4x^2+y^2=4\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(3\\)",
    "E": "\\(4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A common point must satisfy both equations. Multiplying the first equation by \\(4\\) makes its \\(x^2\\) term match the one in the second equation:\n\\[\n4x^2+16y^2=4,\n\\qquad\n4x^2+y^2=4.\n\\]\nSubtracting the second equation from the first eliminates \\(x^2\\) and gives\n\\[\n15y^2=0.\n\\]\nHence \\(y=0\\). Substitution into the original first equation gives \\(x^2=1\\), so \\(x=1\\) or \\(x=-1\\).\n\nBoth \\((1,0)\\) and \\((-1,0)\\) satisfy the original equations. Every common point was forced to be one of these two, and their coordinates differ. Thus there are exactly \\(\\boxed{2}\\) distinct common points, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
